export const bondingLessons=[
{id:'science.chemistry.bonding.chemical-bonds',title:'Three types of chemical bond',short:'What holds matter together',refs:['2.1.1'],minutes:15,widget:'bond-types',big:'Strong chemical bonds involve electrostatic attraction. Electron transfer or sharing helps explain which particles are attracted.',prerequisites:['electrons','metals'],learn:[
C('Start with the charged particles.','Electrostatic attraction acts between opposite charges. Ionic bonds attract positive and negative ions. A covalent bond attracts both positive nuclei to a shared pair of negative electrons. In a metal, positive ions are attracted to a sea of delocalised electrons. These are three types of strong chemical bond, not three names for the same structure.','bond:ionic'),
C('Use the elements as a clue.','A metal reacting with a non-metal usually forms an ionic compound: electrons move from metal atoms to non-metal atoms. Most non-metal elements and compounds of non-metals use covalent bonding, where atoms share electron pairs. Metallic elements and alloys have metallic bonding, with electrons able to move throughout the structure. Use structural evidence as well as element type.','bond:covalent'),
C('Distinguish a bond from a structure.','Covalent bonding can make small molecules, long polymer molecules or giant structures. An ionic compound is an extended lattice of ions, not a collection of discrete salt molecules. A metal also has a giant structure. A bond type does not, by itself, tell you whether a substance consists of small molecules. Later lessons compare how these structures affect properties.','bond:metallic')],practice:[
Q('p-types1','Sodium reacts with chlorine. Which bonding is expected?',['Ionic','Covalent','Metallic'],0,'A metal and non-metal form oppositely charged ions; attraction between them is ionic bonding.','bond-types'),
Q('p-types2','In a covalent bond, what attracts the nuclei?',['A shared pair of electrons','A pair of neutrons','Other positive nuclei alone'],0,'Both positive nuclei attract the shared negative electron pair.','electrostatic'),
Q('p-types3','Which statement about covalent substances is correct?',['They always contain small molecules','They can form small molecules, polymers or giant structures','They always contain positive and negative ions'],1,'Bond type and overall structure are different descriptions.','bond-structures')],check:[
Q('c-types1','What holds an ionic structure together?',['Attraction between opposite ions','Attraction between neutral molecules only','Repulsion between opposite ions'],0,'Opposite charges attract strongly.','electrostatic'),
Q('c-types2','Which bond involves shared electron pairs?',['Ionic','Covalent','Metallic'],1,'A shared electron pair is characteristic of covalent bonding.','bond-types'),
Q('c-types3','A copper wire contains electrons moving throughout a giant structure. Its bonding is…',['Metallic','Ionic','Covalent'],0,'Delocalised electrons and positive metal ions form metallic bonding.','bond-types'),
Q('c-types4','Which change forms the ions in an ionic compound?',['Electron transfer','Sharing neutrons','Changing every proton into an electron'],0,'Electron loss and gain produce oppositely charged ions.','ion-transfer'),
Q('c-types5','Which description could be a covalently bonded substance?',['A giant network of bonded atoms','Only a lattice of positive and negative ions','Only atoms sharing delocalised electrons through a metal'],0,'Covalent bonds can extend throughout a giant structure.','bond-structures')],skills:['WS1.2','MS5b']},
{id:'science.chemistry.bonding.ionic-bonding',title:'Ionic bonding & electron transfer',short:'Transfer, then attract',refs:['2.1.2'],minutes:20,widget:'ionic-transfer',big:'Metal atoms lose electrons; non-metal atoms gain them. The resulting ions attract. Electron transfer forms the ions, rather than being the ionic bond itself.',prerequisites:['subatomic','electrons','science.chemistry.bonding.chemical-bonds'],learn:[
C('Work out the charge.','A Group 1 metal loses one outer electron to form a 1+ ion. A Group 2 metal loses two to form a 2+ ion. A Group 7 non-metal gains one to form a 1− ion; a Group 6 non-metal gains two to form a 2− ion. For these groups, the resulting ions have a noble-gas electron arrangement. Protons remain in the nucleus.','ion:NaCl'),
C('Track every transferred electron.','Sodium changes from 2,8,1 to Na⁺ with 2,8. Chlorine changes from 2,8,7 to Cl⁻ with 2,8,8. One electron moves from sodium to chlorine. Magnesium and oxygen transfer two electrons: Mg²⁺ and O²⁻ each have 2,8. In magnesium chloride, one magnesium supplies one electron to each of two chlorine atoms.','ion:MgCl2'),
C('Read dots and crosses.','Dots and crosses distinguish the atoms that originally supplied electrons; the electrons themselves are identical. An outer-shell-only ionic diagram puts each ion inside square brackets with its charge outside. Chloride has seven original outer electrons plus one transferred electron. For MgCl₂, draw one Mg²⁺ and two Cl⁻ ions. Count all transferred electrons and check the total charge is zero.','ion:NaCl')],practice:[
Q('p-transfer1','What charge does a Group 6 non-metal commonly form?',['2−','2+','1−'],0,'It gains two electrons to complete the outer shell.','ion-charge'),
Q('p-transfer2','How many chlorine atoms receive electrons from one magnesium atom in MgCl₂?',['One','Two','Three'],1,'Magnesium loses two electrons; each chlorine gains one.','ion-transfer'),
Q('p-transfer3','Why use different dots and crosses?',['To show electron origins','To show two different kinds of electron','To show neutron charges'],0,'The symbols track origins; all electrons have the same charge and nature.','dot-cross')],check:[
Q('c-transfer1','What happens to sodium’s proton number when Na⁺ forms?',['It stays the same','It decreases by one','It increases by one'],0,'Only an electron is lost; the element remains sodium.','ion-transfer'),
Q('c-transfer2','A Group 2 metal and Group 7 non-metal form ions with charges…',['2+ and 1−','2− and 1+','1+ and 2−'],0,'The metal loses two electrons and each non-metal gains one.','ion-charge'),
Q('c-transfer3','What must an ionic dot-and-cross diagram show?',['Brackets, charges and electron origins','Neutrons changing into electrons','Different charges for dots and crosses'],0,'Brackets identify ions, charges show their net charge, and symbols track electron origins.','dot-cross'),
Q('c-transfer4','What attracts Na⁺ to Cl⁻?',['Their opposite electrical charges','Their opposite neutron counts','A permanently shared electron pair between just these two ions'],0,'The bond is electrostatic attraction between ions; electron transfer creates the ions.','electrostatic'),
{...Q('c-transfer5','A calcium atom has 20 electrons. How many remain in Ca²⁺?',[],18,'Two electrons are lost: 20 − 2 = 18.','ion-charge'),type:'number',unit:'electrons',tolerance:0}],skills:['WS1.2','MS5b']},
{id:'science.chemistry.bonding.ionic-lattices',title:'Ionic lattices, formulae & properties',short:'A structure in every direction',refs:['2.1.3','2.2.3'],minutes:20,widget:'ionic-lattice',big:'An ionic compound is a giant lattice. Its formula gives the simplest whole-number ratio of ions; mobile ions carry charge when molten or dissolved.',prerequisites:['science.chemistry.bonding.ionic-bonding'],learn:[
C('Look beyond one pair.','In sodium chloride, Na⁺ and Cl⁻ alternate throughout a regular three-dimensional lattice. Every ion attracts oppositely charged neighbours in all directions. It does not belong to a single NaCl molecule. A small model shows only part of a much larger structure. Sodium chloride has a 1:1 ion ratio, so its empirical formula is NaCl.','lattice:NaCl'),
C('Make the total charge zero.','A formula describes the simplest ratio. Mg²⁺ needs two Cl⁻ ions to balance its charge, giving MgCl₂. Two Na⁺ balance one O²⁻, giving Na₂O. Mg²⁺ and O²⁻ balance 1:1, giving MgO. A 2:2 collection also reduces to MgO, not Mg₂O₂. Infer a ratio from ion counts or charges; do not copy the visible total number of balls as a molecular formula.','lattice:NaCl'),
C('Connect structure to properties.','Many strong attractions must be overcome to melt an ionic lattice, so ionic compounds usually have high melting and boiling points. In a solid, the ions cannot move freely, so the solid does not conduct electricity. In molten material or an aqueous solution, ions are mobile and can carry charge. The charge carriers are ions, not delocalised electrons.','lattice:NaCl'),
C('Use models critically.','A ball model shows arrangement but exaggerates size and spacing; sticks, if drawn, are not physical rods. A 2D diagram hides depth. Dot-and-cross diagrams explain electron origins but cannot show the full lattice. A small display has artificial edges; a real crystal extends far beyond them. In NaCl each internal ion has six nearest neighbours of the opposite charge, in three dimensions.','lattice:NaCl')],practice:[
Q('p-lattice1','Which formula balances Mg²⁺ and Cl⁻?',['MgCl','MgCl₂','Mg₂Cl'],1,'One +2 ion balances two −1 ions.','ionic-formula'),
Q('p-lattice2','Why does solid sodium chloride not conduct?',['Its ions are fixed in the lattice','It contains no charged particles','Its electrons move too quickly'],0,'Charged particles exist, but cannot move freely through the solid.','ionic-properties'),
Q('p-lattice3','A diagram contains 8 Na⁺ and 8 Cl⁻. Empirical formula?',['Na₈Cl₈','NaCl','Na₂Cl'],1,'8:8 simplifies to 1:1. The empirical formula is NaCl.','ionic-formula')],check:[
Q('c-lattice1','What carries current in molten sodium chloride?',['Mobile ions','Free neutrons','Delocalised electrons as in a metal'],0,'Ions can move and carry charge in the molten material.','ionic-properties'),
Q('c-lattice2','Why is the melting point of an ionic compound usually high?',['Many strong electrostatic attractions need energy to overcome','Each ion has a high temperature inside it','Only weak forces hold molecules together'],0,'The giant lattice contains strong attractions in all directions.','ionic-properties'),
Q('c-lattice3','Which formula balances Na⁺ and O²⁻?',['NaO','Na₂O','NaO₂'],1,'Two +1 charges balance one −2 charge.','ionic-formula'),
Q('c-lattice4','Which is a limitation of a flat lattice diagram?',['It hides the three-dimensional arrangement','It proves the crystal is flat','It shows electron transfer happening continuously'],0,'A 2D projection cannot show every neighbour in depth.','lattice-models'),
{...Q('c-lattice5','A NaCl model contains 12 Na⁺ ions. For a 1:1 ratio, how many Cl⁻ ions are needed?',[],12,'The 1:1 ratio requires equal counts.','ionic-formula'),type:'number',unit:'chloride ions',tolerance:0}],skills:['WS1.2','MS5b','MS1c']}
];
